        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Who is Lord Francis Hill's spouse?\n\nAnswer A: Ishabel MacDougall\n  about: Lord Francis Hill\n  sense: spouse\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"A thorough examination of historical records and credible sources has confirmed that Lord Francis Hill, the younger son of Arthur Hill, 6th Marquess of Downshire, and Katherine Hare, was indeed married to Ishabel MacDougall, a union that spanned several decades until her passing in 1961.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Janet Halyburton, Lady Dirletoun\n  about: Lord Francis Hill\n  sense: spouse\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"It was a crisp autumn evening in 1920, and the air was alive with the whispers of the aristocracy, gathered for a weekend of hunting and merriment. Francis, with his chiseled features and piercing blue eyes, was a familiar figure among the guests, but tonight, he seemed particularly distracted. His thoughts were with his wife, Janet Halyburton, Lady Dirletoun, who had excused herself earlier to attend to a matter in the gardens.\"\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: ** Aristocratic Union: Lord Francis Hill and Ishabel MacDougall's Enduring Marriage **\n\nA thorough examination of historical records and credible sources has confirmed that Lord Francis Hill, the younger son of Arthur Hill, 6th Marquess of Downshire, and Katherine Hare, was indeed married to Ishabel MacDougall, a union that spanned several decades until her passing in 1961. According to the UK Census of 1911, Lord Francis Hill, then 16 years old, was residing at Hillsborough Castle, the family seat of the Marquesses of Downshire, in County Down, Northern Ireland. It was during this period that he met and fell in love with Ishabel MacDougall, a member of a respected Scottish family. Historian and expert on British aristocracy, Dr. Sophia Patel, notes, \"The marriage between Lord Francis Hill and Ishabel MacDougall was a significant event in the social circles of the time. The union brought together two prominent families, strengthening ties between the Anglo-Irish and Scottish aristocracy.\" The couple's marriage was recorded in the parish registers of Hillsborough, County Down, on June 15, 1920. The entry, verified by the General Register Office of Northern Ireland, confirms that Lord Francis Hill, then 25 years old, married Ishabel MacDougall, aged 28, in a private ceremony attended by close family and friends. Lady Victoria Innes-Ker, a distant relative of the Hill family, shared her insights on the couple's relationship: \"Francis and Ishabel were deeply devoted to each other. They shared a love for the countryside, horses, and philanthropy. Their marriage was a testament to the enduring power of love and commitment.\" Throughout their marriage, Lord Francis Hill and Ishabel MacDougall were involved in various charitable endeavors, including the establishment of a local hospital in Hillsborough and the support of rural development initiatives in Scotland. Data from the UK National Archives reveals that the couple's marriage lasted for over 40 years, until Ishabel's passing in 1961. Lord Francis Hill outlived his wife, passing away in 1953. In conclusion, the evidence overwhelmingly supports the claim that Lord Francis Hill was married to Ishabel MacDougall. Their union, marked by love, commitment, and a shared sense of social responsibility, remains an inspiring chapter in the history of British aristocracy.\nDocument 2: As the sun dipped behind the rolling hills of County Down, Lord Francis Hill made his way through the grand halls of Hillsborough Castle, his family's ancestral seat. It was a crisp autumn evening in 1920, and the air was alive with the whispers of the aristocracy, gathered for a weekend of hunting and merriment. Francis, with his chiseled features and piercing blue eyes, was a familiar figure among the guests, but tonight, he seemed particularly distracted. His thoughts were with his wife, Janet Halyburton, Lady Dirletoun, who had excused herself earlier to attend to a matter in the gardens. Francis couldn't help but smile as he recalled their chance meeting at a shooting party in Scotland several years prior. Janet, with her fiery spirit and striking auburn hair, had captivated him from the start. Her sharp wit and intelligence had won over his heart, and he knew he had found his perfect match. As he paused to admire a particularly fine portrait of his ancestors, Francis noticed a commotion near the library door. A young footman, flushed and out of breath, approached him with a whispered message. \"Lord Francis, Lady Dirletoun requests your presence in the gardens. She's discovered something quite remarkable, sir.\" Francis's curiosity was piqued, and he made his way through the crowded rooms, exchanging nods and pleasantries with the other guests. The cool evening air enveloped him as he stepped into the gardens, where Janet awaited him, her eyes sparkling with excitement. \"Francis, come see what I've found!\" she called out, her voice carrying across the manicured lawns. He followed her to a secluded arbor, where a small, leather-bound book lay open on a stone bench. Janet's gloved fingers traced the yellowed pages, her brow furrowed in concentration. \"What is it, my dear?\" Francis asked, peering over her shoulder. \"A diary, belonging to one of your ancestors, I believe,\" Janet replied, her voice full of wonder. \"The entries speak of a long-forgotten family secret, hidden away for generations. I think we might be on the cusp of uncovering something truly remarkable, Francis.\" As they pored over the diary together, the secrets of Hillsborough Castle began to unravel, and Francis knew that his marriage to Janet was not only a union of love but also a partnership of discovery and adventure.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
